    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes, from a baseline numbers perspective, it is a solid offer.** \nFor a Google L6 Senior Product Manager in the Bay Area, this offer is around the 70th\u201375th percentile for an initial package:\n\n* **Total Compensation (TC) Reality:** While straight-line annualized TC appears to be **$552,000** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k/yr stock), Google typically uses **front-loaded vesting** (38% Year 1, 32% Year 2, 20% Year 3, 10% Year 4, or in some cases 33/33/22/12). \n  * Under the 38/32/20/10 schedule, your **Year 1 TC is ~$692,400** ($235k base + $47k target bonus + $410.4k stock).\n  * Under a 33/33/22/12 schedule, your **Year 1 TC is ~$638,400**.\n* **Comparison to Meta:** Both scenarios represent a meaningful cash/stock bump over your current ~$526,000/yr total compensation.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Gaps and Risks in the Offer\n\nDespite the strong Year 1 headline number, there are three clear areas where you are leaving money on the table:\n\n1. **Lower Base Salary:** You are currently making $240,000 base at Meta. Google's offer of $235,000 represents a base pay cut, even though their disclosed range goes up to **$271,000**.\n2. **$0 Sign-On Bonus:** Standard Google L6 offers for passively sourced candidates leaving competitive Big Tech roles almost always include a sign-on bonus ($50,000\u2013$100,000+) to offset leaving unvested equity.\n3. **The Year 3\u20134 Equity Cliff:** Because of Google's front-loaded vesting, your stock income from this grant drops from ~$410k in Year 1 to ~$108k in Year 4. While annual refreshers stack, they depend on performance ratings and rarely fill the entire gap without top-tier reviews.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should you accept, negotiate, or walk away?\n\n**Negotiate.** Do not accept the initial offer as-is, and do not walk away yet.\n\nYou have maximum leverage:\n* You were **passively sourced** and are happy in your current role.\n* You are already performing at the equivalent level (Meta IC6 $\\leftrightarrow$ Google L6).\n* Google knows what Meta pays and has room within their bands to improve the package.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What exactly to ask for and how much\n\nAnchor your counter around:\n1. Not taking a step down in guaranteed base pay.\n2. Bridge compensation (sign-on bonus) for leaving unvested Meta RSUs.\n3. Aligning with top-of-band equity for L6.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Your Counter Target | Realistic Landing Zone |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n| **4-Yr RSUs** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | 20% ($47k) | Fixed by level (20%) | 20% (unchanged) |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Strategy\n\nDeliver this verbally to your recruiter first, followed by a brief summary email:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the role. However, as I shared earlier, I wasn't actively looking and I\u2019m in a very strong position at Meta where I'm currently tracking around $526k/year with $200k in equity vesting over the next 12 months.*\n>\n> *To make the transition compelling and offset what I am walking away from, I\u2019d like to see if we can adjust three areas:*\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** Since my current base is $240k, I\u2019d like to move to **$255,000** (well within your posted range).\n> 2. * **Sign-on Bonus:** To bridge the transition and offset unvested equity, I\u2019m looking for a **$80,000\u2013$100,000 sign-on bonus**.\n> 3. * **Equity:** I'd like to bring the equity grant to **$1,250,000 over 4 years**.\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be ready to sign and accept.\"*\n\n### Key Non-Financial Consideration\nBefore deciding, evaluate **organizational momentum and PM culture**. Meta is notoriously execution-heavy with high PM autonomy and speed. Google PM roles tend to be more consensus-driven and matrixed, with heavier engineering and cross-functional buy-in required to ship. Ensure the specific team/org (e.g., Gemini/Labs vs. Ads/Cloud) matches your working style and career goals.",
