    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Numbers on the Revised Offer\n\nGoogle responded directly to your leverage by matching your base and increasing your equity grant by **$200,000** (from $1.08M to $1.28M). \n\nBecause Google uses front-loaded vesting (typically **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** or **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**), this equity bump dramatically increases your immediate cash flow:\n\n| Metric | Your Current Meta (IC6) | Google Revised Offer (38/32/20/10) | Google Revised Offer (33/33/22/12) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) |\n| **Year 1 Stock Vest** | ~$200,000 \u2013 $250,000 | **$486,400** (38%) | **$422,400** (33%) |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **$774,400** | **$710,400** |\n| **4-Year Annualized TC** | ~$526,000 | **$608,000 / yr** | **$608,000 / yr** |\n\n#### The Sign-On vs. Equity Tradeoff\nWhile Google did not provide a separate cash sign-on bonus, **they effectively rolled it into your equity grant**. \n* You told them you were walking away from $200k of Meta stock over the next 12 months.\n* By adding $200k to your total grant, they increased your **Year 1 stock payout by $66k\u2013$76k** and your **Year 2 payout by $64k\u2013$66k**.\n* In Year 1 alone, you will earn **~$184,000 to $248,000 more** than you would make by staying at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is this truly their \"best offer\"?\n\n**Yes, for all practical purposes.**\nAn initial RSU grant of **$1,280,000** places this offer in the **top 10% (90th+ percentile)** for Google L6 PM roles in the Bay Area. Without a competing offer in hand (e.g., a counter-offer from Apple, Amazon, or a formal retention package from Meta), Google's compensation committee will not move the needle any further. Pushing again on numbers risks fatiguing the team without yielding additional gains.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Take It?\n\nFrom a purely financial standpoint, **yes, this is an exceptional offer and a strong upgrade.**\n\nHowever, since you mentioned you were happy at Meta and not looking, use this decision framework:\n\n#### Reasons to Take the Offer:\n1. **Immediate Financial Arbitrage:** You earn an extra ~$180k\u2013$250k in Year 1 and an extra ~$170k in Year 2 compared to staying at Meta.\n2. **Career Diversification:** Having both Meta (IC6) and Google (L6) Product Management on your resume cements you at the Staff/Principal tier across top-tier tech for future executive recruiting.\n3. **Pace & Sustainability:** If you ever felt Meta's performance culture (e.g., PSC cycles, reorgs, intense top-down execution) was draining, Google typically offers better work-life balance and psychological safety, while paying you significantly more.\n\n#### Reasons to Walk Away (Stay at Meta):\n1. **Product Velocity & Culture Mismatch:** \n   * **Meta PMs** operate with high autonomy, bias toward fast execution, and a culture where PMs strongly direct product strategy.\n   * **Google PMs** face a much more consensus-driven, matrixed, and engineering-centric environment where getting alignment across cross-functional teams and VPs can take significantly longer. If you thrive on shipping fast with minimal red tape, Google can feel frustrating.\n2. **Your Meta Trajectory:** If you are well-positioned for an **IC7 (Director/Principal PM)** promotion at Meta in the next 12\u201318 months, an IC7 band at Meta will out-earn Google L6 in years 3\u20134.\n\n---\n\n### Final Recommendation\n\n* **If you are excited about the team/charter at Google and open to a new environment:** **Accept the offer.** It is near top-of-band for L6 and makes your transition well worth it financially.\n* **If you love your current team/momentum at Meta and dread slow consensus-driven cultures:** **Politely decline.** You negotiated a top-tier package, gave it honest consideration, and can remain in a role where you are thriving.",
